{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The server entry point is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"optional"}]},{"type":"text","value":" out of the box. If not provided, TanStack Start will automatically handle the server entry point for you using the below as a default."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Server Entry Point supports the universal fetch handler format, commonly used by "},{"type":"link","href":"https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/handlers/fetch/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cloudflare Workers"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and other WinterCG-compatible runtimes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To ensure interoperability, the default export must conform to our "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ServerEntry"},{"type":"text","value":" interface:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"export default {\n  fetch(req: Request, opts?: RequestOptions): Response | Promise<Response> {\n    // ...\n  },\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Start exposes a wrapper to make creation type-safe. This is done in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"src/server.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" file."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// src/server.ts\nimport handler, { createServerEntry } from '@tanstack/react-start/server-entry'\n\nexport default createServerEntry({\n  fetch(request) {\n    return handler.fetch(request)\n  },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Whether we are statically generating our app or serving it dynamically, the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"server.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" file is the entry point for doing all SSR-related work as well as for handling server routes and server function requests."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"custom-server-handlers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom Server Handlers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can create custom server handlers to modify how your application is rendered:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// src/server.ts\nimport {\n  createStartHandler,\n  defaultStreamHandler,\n  defineHandlerCallback,\n} from '@tanstack/react-start/server'\nimport { createServerEntry } from '@tanstack/react-start/server-entry'\n\nconst customHandler = defineHandlerCallback((ctx) => {\n  // add custom logic here\n  return defaultStreamHandler(ctx)\n})\n\nconst fetch = createStartHandler(customHandler)\n\nexport default createServerEntry({\n  fetch,\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"request-context","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Request context"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When your server needs to pass additional, typed data into request handlers (for example, authenticated user info, a database connection, or per-request flags), register a request context type via TypeScript module augmentation. The registered context is delivered as the second argument to the server "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetch"},{"type":"text","value":" handler and is available throughout the server-side middleware chain — including global middleware, request/function middleware, server routes, server functions, and the router itself."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To add types for your request context, augment the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Register"},{"type":"text","value":" interface from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/react-router"},{"type":"text","value":" with a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"server.requestContext"},{"type":"text","value":" property. The runtime "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context"},{"type":"text","value":" you pass to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"handler.fetch"},{"type":"text","value":" will then match that type. Example:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import handler, { createServerEntry } from '@tanstack/react-start/server-entry'\n\ntype MyRequestContext = {\n  hello: string\n  foo: number\n}\n\ndeclare module '@tanstack/react-router' {\n  interface Register {\n    server: {\n      requestContext: MyRequestContext\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nexport default createServerEntry({\n  async fetch(request) {\n    return handler.fetch(request, { context: { hello: 'world', foo: 123 } })\n  },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"server-configuration","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server Configuration"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The server entry point is where you can configure server-specific behavior:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Request/response middleware"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom error handling"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Authentication logic"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Database connections"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Logging and monitoring"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This flexibility allows you to customize how your TanStack Start application handles server-side rendering while maintaining the framework's conventions."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"cloudflare-workers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cloudflare Workers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When deploying to Cloudflare Workers, you can extend "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"server.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" to handle additional Workers features like queues, scheduled events, and Durable Objects. For a comprehensive guide, see the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/framework-guides/web-apps/tanstack-start/#custom-entrypoints","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cloudflare Workers documentation for TanStack Start"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"custom-server-handlers","text":"Custom Server Handlers","level":2},{"id":"request-context","text":"Request context","level":2},{"id":"server-configuration","text":"Server Configuration","level":2},{"id":"cloudflare-workers","text":"Cloudflare Workers","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n> [!NOTE]\n> The server entry point is **optional** out of the box. If not provided, TanStack Start will automatically handle the server entry point for you using the below as a default.\n\nThe Server Entry Point supports the universal fetch handler format, commonly used by [Cloudflare Workers](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/handlers/fetch/) and other WinterCG-compatible runtimes.\n\nTo ensure interoperability, the default export must conform to our `ServerEntry` interface:\n\n```ts\nexport default {\n  fetch(req: Request, opts?: RequestOptions): Response | Promise<Response> {\n    // ...\n  },\n}\n```\n\nTanStack Start exposes a wrapper to make creation type-safe. This is done in the `src/server.ts` file.\n\n```tsx\n// src/server.ts\nimport handler, { createServerEntry } from '@tanstack/react-start/server-entry'\n\nexport default createServerEntry({\n  fetch(request) {\n    return handler.fetch(request)\n  },\n})\n```\n\nWhether we are statically generating our app or serving it dynamically, the `server.ts` file is the entry point for doing all SSR-related work as well as for handling server routes and server function requests.\n\n## Custom Server Handlers\n\nYou can create custom server handlers to modify how your application is rendered:\n\n```tsx\n// src/server.ts\nimport {\n  createStartHandler,\n  defaultStreamHandler,\n  defineHandlerCallback,\n} from '@tanstack/react-start/server'\nimport { createServerEntry } from '@tanstack/react-start/server-entry'\n\nconst customHandler = defineHandlerCallback((ctx) => {\n  // add custom logic here\n  return defaultStreamHandler(ctx)\n})\n\nconst fetch = createStartHandler(customHandler)\n\nexport default createServerEntry({\n  fetch,\n})\n```\n\n## Request context\n\nWhen your server needs to pass additional, typed data into request handlers (for example, authenticated user info, a database connection, or per-request flags), register a request context type via TypeScript module augmentation. The registered context is delivered as the second argument to the server `fetch` handler and is available throughout the server-side middleware chain — including global middleware, request/function middleware, server routes, server functions, and the router itself.\n\nTo add types for your request context, augment the `Register` interface from `@tanstack/react-router` with a `server.requestContext` property. The runtime `context` you pass to `handler.fetch` will then match that type. Example:\n\n```tsx\nimport handler, { createServerEntry } from '@tanstack/react-start/server-entry'\n\ntype MyRequestContext = {\n  hello: string\n  foo: number\n}\n\ndeclare module '@tanstack/react-router' {\n  interface Register {\n    server: {\n      requestContext: MyRequestContext\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nexport default createServerEntry({\n  async fetch(request) {\n    return handler.fetch(request, { context: { hello: 'world', foo: 123 } })\n  },\n})\n```\n\n## Server Configuration\n\nThe server entry point is where you can configure server-specific behavior:\n\n- Request/response middleware\n- Custom error handling\n- Authentication logic\n- Database connections\n- Logging and monitoring\n\nThis flexibility allows you to customize how your TanStack Start application handles server-side rendering while maintaining the framework's conventions.\n\n## Cloudflare Workers\n\nWhen deploying to Cloudflare Workers, you can extend `server.ts` to handle additional Workers features like queues, scheduled events, and Durable Objects. For a comprehensive guide, see the [Cloudflare Workers documentation for TanStack Start](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/framework-guides/web-apps/tanstack-start/#custom-entrypoints).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: server-entry-point\ntitle: Server Entry Point\n---\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> The server entry point is **optional** out of the box. If not provided, TanStack Start will automatically handle the server entry point for you using the below as a default.\n\nThe Server Entry Point supports the universal fetch handler format, commonly used by [Cloudflare Workers](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/handlers/fetch/) and other WinterCG-compatible runtimes.\n\nTo ensure interoperability, the default export must conform to our `ServerEntry` interface:\n\n```ts\nexport default {\n  fetch(req: Request, opts?: RequestOptions): Response | Promise<Response> {\n    // ...\n  },\n}\n```\n\nTanStack Start exposes a wrapper to make creation type-safe. This is done in the `src/server.ts` file.\n\n```tsx\n// src/server.ts\nimport handler, { createServerEntry } from '@tanstack/react-start/server-entry'\n\nexport default createServerEntry({\n  fetch(request) {\n    return handler.fetch(request)\n  },\n})\n```\n\nWhether we are statically generating our app or serving it dynamically, the `server.ts` file is the entry point for doing all SSR-related work as well as for handling server routes and server function requests.\n\n## Custom Server Handlers\n\nYou can create custom server handlers to modify how your application is rendered:\n\n```tsx\n// src/server.ts\nimport {\n  createStartHandler,\n  defaultStreamHandler,\n  defineHandlerCallback,\n} from '@tanstack/react-start/server'\nimport { createServerEntry } from '@tanstack/react-start/server-entry'\n\nconst customHandler = defineHandlerCallback((ctx) => {\n  // add custom logic here\n  return defaultStreamHandler(ctx)\n})\n\nconst fetch = createStartHandler(customHandler)\n\nexport default createServerEntry({\n  fetch,\n})\n```\n\n## Request context\n\nWhen your server needs to pass additional, typed data into request handlers (for example, authenticated user info, a database connection, or per-request flags), register a request context type via TypeScript module augmentation. The registered context is delivered as the second argument to the server `fetch` handler and is available throughout the server-side middleware chain — including global middleware, request/function middleware, server routes, server functions, and the router itself.\n\nTo add types for your request context, augment the `Register` interface from `@tanstack/react-router` with a `server.requestContext` property. The runtime `context` you pass to `handler.fetch` will then match that type. Example:\n\n```tsx\nimport handler, { createServerEntry } from '@tanstack/react-start/server-entry'\n\ntype MyRequestContext = {\n  hello: string\n  foo: number\n}\n\ndeclare module '@tanstack/react-router' {\n  interface Register {\n    server: {\n      requestContext: MyRequestContext\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nexport default createServerEntry({\n  async fetch(request) {\n    return handler.fetch(request, { context: { hello: 'world', foo: 123 } })\n  },\n})\n```\n\n## Server Configuration\n\nThe server entry point is where you can configure server-specific behavior:\n\n- Request/response middleware\n- Custom error handling\n- Authentication logic\n- Database connections\n- Logging and monitoring\n\nThis flexibility allows you to customize how your TanStack Start application handles server-side rendering while maintaining the framework's conventions.\n\n## Cloudflare Workers\n\nWhen deploying to Cloudflare Workers, you can extend `server.ts` to handle additional Workers features like queues, scheduled events, and Durable Objects. For a comprehensive guide, see the [Cloudflare Workers documentation for TanStack Start](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/framework-guides/web-apps/tanstack-start/#custom-entrypoints).\n"}
